{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses into the very induction it seeks to avoid because the 'methodological rule' to prefer better-corroborated theories for 'rapid error elimination' implicitly assumes that a theory which survived severe tests in the past is more likely to yield informative (i.e., non-refuted) results in the future than an untested one. If there is no inductive link between past survival and future performance, the choice between a well-corroborated theory and a new, bold conjecture is methodologically neutral regarding 'error elimination'; preferring the former solely because it worked before is an inductive wager on nature's uniformity, smuggling back the principle Popper rejected.","premise":"A methodological rule that selects Theory A over Theory B based on past test performance necessarily presupposes that past performance is a reliable guide to future utility, which is the definition of inductive reasoning.","premises_essential":["b4ea2311d93fe2e3993c1704bc61262fb2ef15fc2493cb515be6380ef5470f64"],"successor_question":"If corroboration provides no basis for expecting future success, on what non-inductive grounds does Popper claim that choosing a well-corroborated theory maximizes 'informational content' or aids 'error elimination' compared to choosing a random untested theory?","target":"b4ea2311d93fe2e3993c1704bc61262fb2ef15fc2493cb515be6380ef5470f64"}]}